complete_reproduction_plan:
  paper_info:
    title: "Neural Posterior Score Estimation for Simulation-Based Inference"
    core_contribution: >
      Reproduce NPSE (non-sequential neural posterior score estimation) and TSNPSE
      (truncated sequential neural posterior score estimation) using time-varying
      score-based diffusion models, together with NLSE, SNPSE-A/B/C variants,
      benchmark experiments, and the pyloric-network application.

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # SECTION 1: FILE STRUCTURE DESIGN
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  file_structure: |
    npse/
    ├── README.md                         # created LAST
    ├── requirements.txt                  # created LAST
    ├── config/
    │   ├── default.yaml                  # hyperparameters, SDE choices, budgets
    │   └── benchmarks.yaml               # benchmark-specific settings
    ├── src/
    │   ├── __init__.py
    │   ├── sde.py                        # VE/VP SDE definitions and transition kernels
    │   ├── networks.py                   # theta/x embeddings, time embedding, score nets
    │   ├── losses.py                     # DSM losses for NPSE, NLSE, prior, SNPSE variants
    │   ├── sampling.py                   # probability flow ODE sampler
    │   ├── density.py                    # instantaneous change-of-variables density evaluation
    │   ├── npse.py                       # non-sequential NPSE trainer
    │   ├── tsnpse.py                     # truncated sequential TSNPSE trainer
    │   ├── snpse.py                      # SNPSE-A, SNPSE-B, SNPSE-C
    │   ├── nlse.py                       # Neural Likelihood Score Estimation
    │   ├── prior.py                      # exact/estimated perturbed prior scores
    │   ├── hpr.py                        # HPR truncation threshold and truncated proposal sampler
    │   └── metrics.py                    # C2ST, MMD, coverage
    ├── benchmarks/
    │   ├── __init__.py
    │   ├── base.py
    │   ├── gaussian_linear.py
    │   ├── gaussian_mixture.py
    │   ├── two_moons.py
    │   ├── gaussian_linear_uniform.py
    │   ├── bernoulli_glm.py
    │   ├── slcp.py
    │   ├── sir.py
    │   └── lotka_volterra.py
    ├── experiments/
    │   ├── train_npse_benchmarks.py      # reproduce Figure 2 / Appendix F
    │   ├── train_tsnpse_benchmarks.py    # reproduce Figure 3
    │   ├── run_snpse_variants.py         # reproduce Figure 6
    │   ├── run_nlse_comparison.py        # reproduce Figure 5
    │   ├── run_pyloric.py                # reproduce Figure 4
    │   ├── evaluate_benchmarks.py
    │   └── make_figures.py
    └── tests/
        ├── test_sde.py
        ├── test_networks.py
        ├── test_losses.py
        ├── test_sampling.py
        └── test_benchmarks.py

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # SECTION 2: IMPLEMENTATION COMPONENTS
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  implementation_components: |
    COMPONENT 1: Forward and Reverse SDE machinery
    - File: src/sde.py
    - Implement a base SDE class with:
        f(theta, t), g(t), transition_sample(theta0, t), transition_score(theta_t, theta0, t)
    - VE SDE:
        dθ_t = σ_min * (σ_min/σ_max)^t * sqrt(2 * log(σ_max/σ_min)) dW_t
        transition: p_{t|0}(θ_t | θ0) = N(θ_t | θ0, σ_min² * (σ_max/σ_min)^(2t) I)
        target score: ∇_θ log p_{t|0} = -(θ_t - θ0) / variance
        settings: σ_min=0.01 for 2-D tasks, SIR, Two Moons; σ_min=0.05 otherwise.
        σ_max chosen by Technique 1 from Song & Ermon (2020).
    - VP SDE:
        dθ_t = -0.5 β_t θ_t dt + sqrt(β_t) dW_t
        β_t = β_min + t*(β_max - β_min), β_min=0.1, β_max=11.0
        μ_t = exp(-0.5 * ∫_0^t β_s ds) * θ0
        Σ_t = (1 - exp(-∫_0^t β_s ds)) I
        transition: N(θ_t | μ_t, Σ_t)
        target score: ∇_θ log p_{t|0} = -(θ_t - μ_t) / Σ_t
    - Reverse SDE:
        dθ_t = [f(θ_t,t) - g(t)² ∇_θ log p_t(θ_t|x)] dt + g(t) dW̄_t
    - Probability flow ODE:
        dθ_t = [f(θ_t,t) - 0.5 g(t)² ∇_θ log p_t(θ_t|x)] dt
      Used for all sampling by solving from t=T to t=0 with RK45.

    COMPONENT 2: Score network architecture
    - File: src/networks.py
    - Theta embedding:
        3-layer fully-connected MLP, 256 hidden units.
        input dim = d, output dim = max(30, 4*d).
    - x embedding:
        3-layer fully-connected MLP, 256 hidden units.
        input dim = p, output dim = max(30, 4*p).
    - Time sinusiodal embedding:
        d_emb = 64.
        (t_emb)_i = sin(t / 10000^((i-1)/31)) for i<=32,
        cos(t / 10000^(((i-32)-1)/31)) for i>32.
    - Final score MLP:
        concatenate [theta_emb, x_emb, t_emb],
        3-layer MLP, 256 hidden units, output dim d.
    - All MLPs use SiLU activations.
    - Standardization must be implemented: subtract per-dimension mean and divide by
      standard deviation for both θ_t and x before feeding the network.

    COMPONENT 3: NPSE denoising posterior score matching loss
    - File: src/losses.py
    - For each training sample:
        θ0 ~ p(θ), x ~ p(x|θ), t ~ U(0,T), θ_t ~ p_{t|0}(θ_t|θ0)
    - Objective:
        J_NPSE_DSM(ψ) = 1/2 ∫_0^T λ_t E[ ||s_ψ(θ_t,x,t) - ∇_θ log p_{t|0}(θ_t|θ0)||² ] dt
    - Use λ_t = g(t)² as a sensible default if the paper does not specify another
      value in the accessed text.
    - At the optimum, s_ψ(θ_t,x,t) equals posterior score ∇_θ log p_t(θ_t|x).
      This is proven in Appendix A.1 and implemented directly by the MSE objective.

    COMPONENT 4: Probability flow ODE sampling and density evaluation
    - Files: src/sampling.py, src/density.py
    - Sampling:
        θ_T ~ N(0, I)
        solve probability flow ODE from t=T down to t=0 using RK45.
        Velocity: v(θ,t) = f(θ,t) - 0.5 g(t)² s_ψ(θ,x_obs,t)
    - Density evaluation:
        use instantaneous change-of-variables formula:
          log p_0(θ0|x) = log p_T(θT) + ∫_0^T ∇·v(θ_t,t) dt
        implement using torchdiffeq augmented ODE and Hutchinson trace estimator
        for the divergence.
        This density is required for HPR estimation and truncated proposal sampling.

    COMPONENT 5: Non-sequential NPSE trainer
    - File: src/npse.py
    - Config:
        optimizer: Adam, lr=1e-4.
        validation split: 15%.
        early stopping: stop if validation loss does not decrease for 1000 steps.
        maximum training steps: 3000.
        batch sizes:
          non-sequential budgets 1000/10000: batch size 50.
          budget 100000: batch size 500.
    - Training loop:
        generate/load param-simulator pairs,
        sample times and perturbed parameters,
        compute denoising posterior score matching loss,
        evaluate validation loss,
        return best checkpoint.
    - After training, sample approximate posteriors by solving the probability flow ODE.

    COMPONENT 6: TSNPSE sequential trainer
    - File: src/tsnpse.py
    - Sequential rounds R=10 by default.
    - Per-round simulation budget M = N/R.
    - Proposal prior at round r:
        p̃^r(θ) = (1/r) ∑_{s=0}^{r-1} p_ψ^s(θ | x_obs)
        or, equivalently, the truncated-mixture construction from Proposition C.1:
        p̃^r(θ) ∝ c^r(θ) p(θ), c^r(θ) = (1/r) ∑_{s=0}^{r-1} 1{θ ∈ Θ^s}
      where Θ^0 = support of p(θ), and for s≥1
        Θ^s = HPR_ε(p_ψ^s(θ|x_obs)).
    - Training objective:
        J_TSNPSE(ψ) = 1/2 ∫ λ_t E_{p̃^r(θ0) p(x|θ0) p_{t|0}(θ_t|θ0)}
          [ ||s_ψ(θ_t,x,t) - ∇_θ log p_{t|0}(θ_t|θ0)||² ] dt
    - At test observation x_obs, sampling uses trained score s_ψ(θ_t,x_obs,t) directly.
    - File src/hpr.py:
        HPR truncation threshold ε = 5e-4.
        Draw 20000 approximate posterior samples.
        Compute their log densities with instantaneous change-of-variables.
        Set threshold κ = ε-quantile of log densities.
        Sample truncated proposal by rejection sampling:
          (i) sample θ ~ p(θ),
          (ii) cheap hypercube pre-rejection using posterior-sample min/max,
          (iii) evaluate approximate posterior log density,
          (iv) accept if log density > κ.
    - Accumulate all rounds' samples and retrain from scratch on the combined dataset.

    COMPONENT 7: SNPSE variants
    - File: src/snpse.py
    - SNPSE-A:
        Learn proposal posterior score s̃_ψ^r(θ_t,x,t) from proposal-prior samples.
        After ODE sampling from the proposal posterior, apply Sampling-Importance-
        Resampling (SIR) with importance weights:
          h_i^r = p(θ_i) / p̃^r(θ_i)
        Requires proposal prior density evaluation or approximation.
    - SNPSE-B:
        Directly learn posterior score by reweighting each θ0 sample in the loss by
          w_i = p(θ0) / p̃^r(θ0)
        Objective:
          J = 1/2 ∫ λ_t E_{p̃^r(θ0)} [ w(θ0) ||s_ψ(θ_t,x,t) - ∇_θ log p_{t|0}(θ_t|θ0)||² ]
    - SNPSE-C:
        Learn proposal posterior score using decomposed parameterization:
          s̃_ψ^r(θ_t,x,t) = s_ψ(θ_t,x,t) + ∇_θ log p̃_t^r(θ_t) - ∇_θ log p_t(θ_t)
        where proposal-prior score is estimated via Algorithm 2 or closed-form
        perturbed priors. The paper notes SNPSE-C frequently underperformed,
        so implement for completeness but prioritize TSNPSE.

    COMPONENT 8: Prior score handling for NLSE and SNPSE-C
    - File: src/prior.py
    - Exact perturbed prior score under VE SDE:
        uniform prior:
          p_t(θ_t) ∝ ∏_i [ Φ(b_i | θ_t,i, τ_t²) - Φ(a_i | θ_t,i, τ_t²) ]
        Gaussian mixture prior:
          p_t(θ_t) = ∑ α_i N(θ_t | μ_i, Σ_i + τ_t² I)
        compute gradient with automatic differentiation.
    - If prior is implicit, train score network s_ψ_prior(θ_t,t) by prior denoising
      score matching:
        J_pri = 1/2 ∫ λ_t E_{p(θ0)p_{t|0}} [ ||s_ψ_pri(θ_t,t) - ∇_θ log p_{t|0}(θ_t|θ0)||² ] dt

    COMPONENT 9: NLSE Neural Likelihood Score Estimation
    - File: src/nlse.py
    - Decomposition:
        ∇_θ log p_t(θ_t|x) = ∇_θ log p_t(x|θ_t) + ∇_θ log p_t(θ_t)
    - Learn s_ψ_lik(θ_t,x,t) ≈ ∇_θ log p_t(x|θ_t) using:
        J_lik_DSM = 1/2 ∫ λ_t E[ ||s_ψ_lik + ∇_θ log p_t(θ_t) - ∇_θ log p_{t|0}(θ_t|θ0)||² ] dt
    - Final posterior score:
        s_ψ_post = s_ψ_lik + ∇_θ log p_t(θ_t)

    COMPONENT 10: Multiple-observation bridge posterior
    - Implement bridge score:
        ∇_θ log p_t^bridge(θ_t|x^1,...,x^n)
          = (1-n) ∇_θ log p_t(θ_t) + Σ_{i=1}^n s_ψ(θ_t,x^i,t)
      at each t during reverse sampling.
    - Use annealed MCMC sampling rather than naive reverse-SDE integration because
      intermediate bridge densities differ from true multi-observation posteriors.

    COMPONENT 11: Benchmark simulators
    - Directory: benchmarks/
    - Gaussian Linear:
        θ∈R^10, p(θ)=N(0,0.1 I), p(x|θ)=N(θ,0.1 I), x∈R^10.
    - Gaussian Mixture:
        θ∈R², p(θ)=U(-10,10)², p(x|θ)=0.5 N(x|θ,I)+0.5 N(x|θ,0.01 I).
    - Two Moons:
        θ∈R², p(θ)=U(-1,1)²,
        x = [r cos α + 0.25; r sin α] + [-|θ₁+θ₂|/√2; (-θ₁+θ₂)/√2],
        α~U(-π/2,π/2), r~N(0.1,0.01²).
    - Gaussian Linear Uniform:
        θ∈R¹⁰, p(θ)=U(-1,1)¹⁰, p(x|θ)=N(θ,0.1 I).
    - Bernoulli GLM:
        θ=(β,f)∈R¹⁰; β~N(0,2), f~N(0,(FᵀF)⁻¹),
        F penalizes second-order differences; x∈R¹⁰ are sufficient statistics.
    - SLCP:
        θ∈R⁵ with p(θ)=U(-3,3)⁵; x∈R⁸ from Gaussian whose mean and covariance are
        nonlinear functions of θ, producing four symmetrical posterior modes.
        Use sbibm reference implementation if available.
    - SIR:
        θ=(β,γ), β~LogNormal(log 0.4,0.5), γ~LogNormal(log 0.8,0.2);
        simulate SIR ODEs and observe 10 noisy Binomial recordings from I/N.
    - Lotka Volterra:
        θ=(α,β,γ,δ); α,γ~LogNormal(-0.125,0.5); β,δ~LogNormal(-3,0.5);
        observe 10 recordings of predator and prey populations from ODEs.

    COMPONENT 12: Evaluation metrics
    - File: src/metrics.py
    - Classifier 2-Sample Test (C2ST):
        train an MLP classifier to distinguish approximate posterior samples from
        reference posterior samples; report accuracy, ideally near 0.5 for good fit.
    - Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) as optional secondary metric.
    - Posterior predictive checks and simulation-based coverage calibration (SBCC)
      for the pyloric experiment.

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # SECTION 3: VALIDATION & EVALUATION
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  validation_approach: |
    STAGE 1: Unit validation
    - Verify VE/VP transition means and variances match closed forms.
    - Verify denoising score matching recovers the exact score for a Gaussian target.
    - Verify probability flow ODE sampling yields samples with moments matching
      an analytically tractable Gaussian posterior in Gaussian Linear.
    - Verify instantaneous change-of-variables density matches analytic density.

    STAGE 2: Non-sequential benchmark reproduction
    - Run NPSE on all 8 benchmarks for budgets 1000, 10000, 100000.
    - Use sbibm reference posterior samples and C2ST as main metric.
    - Expected behavior from paper:
        NPSE provides accurate and robust alternatives to NPE.
        Particularly strong on SLCP and Lotka Volterra.
        Best method varies by task for remaining benchmarks.
    - Reproduce Figure 2 and Appendix F comparison to FMPE qualitatively.

    STAGE 3: Sequential benchmark reproduction
    - Run TSNPSE with R=10 rounds, equally divided budgets.
    - At each round:
        estimate HPR_ε using ε=5e-4,
        sample truncated proposal,
        simulate new data,
        retrain score network on accumulated data.
    - Compare with SNPE-C and TSNPE baselines from sbibm.
    - Expected behavior:
        TSNPSE outperforms competitors on SLCP and Lotka Volterra,
        mixed results elsewhere.
    - Reproduce Figure 3 and Table-style C2ST comparisons.

    STAGE 4: Variants and ablations
    - Implement SNPSE-A, SNPSE-B, and optionally SNPSE-C.
    - Reproduce Figure 6: TSNPSE outperforms SNPSE-A and SNPSE-B on SLCP and GLU.
    - Reproduce Figure 5 NPSE vs NLSE on four benchmarks where exact prior score
      is available under VE SDE.

    STAGE 5: Real-world pyloric experiment (optional / if data available)
    - Use 31-parameter simulator, 18 summary statistics, uniform prior.
    - Replace invalid summary statistics with two standard deviations below prior
      predictive mean.
    - Run 9 rounds, 30000 initial simulations, 20000 additional simulations per round.
    - Check final valid summary-statistic percentage reaches about 81%.
    - Posterior predictive sample should closely match observed data.
    - Reproduce Figure 4 and Figure 7 marginal plots qualitatively.

    SUCCESS CRITERIA:
    - C2ST on Gaussian Linear near 0.5.
    - For all benchmarks, C2ST decreases with simulation budget.
    - TSNPSE achieves lower C2ST than non-sequential NPSE on hard benchmarks.
    - Final pyloric run reaches at least ~80% valid summary statistics.

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # SECTION 4: ENVIRONMENT & DEPENDENCIES
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  environment_setup: |
    Language:
      Python 3.9 or 3.10 recommended.

    Core dependencies:
      torch >= 1.13
      torchdiffeq / torch-diffeq for ODE integration
      sbibm for benchmark simulators, reference posterior samples, and baselines
      numpy
      scipy
      scikit-learn for C2ST classifier
      matplotlib
      pandas
      pyyaml
      tqdm

    Optional for pyloric experiment:
      custom pyloric simulator package and observed data files.

    Hardware:
      GPU recommended, especially for budgets 100000.
      CPU is feasible for small budgets and low-dimensional benchmarks,
      but ODE-based density evaluation is expensive.

    Installation notes:
      - sbibm is required mainly for reference tasks and posterior references.
      - torchdiffeq is used for probability flow ODE and change-of-variables density.
      - Pin torch and torchdiffeq versions that are mutually compatible.

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # SECTION 5: IMPLEMENTATION STRATEGY
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  implementation_strategy: |
    PHASE 1: Core SDE and network foundations
    - Implement src/sde.py and src/networks.py first.
    - Add tests/test_sde.py and tests/test_networks.py to validate transitions,
      scores, and output shapes.
    - This phase is dependency-free and must be completed before any training.

    PHASE 2: NPSE loss and non-sequential training
    - Implement src/losses.py and src/npse.py.
    - Implement probability flow ODE sampler in src/sampling.py.
    - Train on Gaussian Linear where the posterior is analytically known.
    - Validate posterior mean/covariance and terminate phase only when close.

    PHASE 3: Benchmarks and evaluation
    - Implement benchmarks/ package, starting with Gaussian Linear, Gaussian Mixture,
      Two Moons, Gaussian Linear Uniform, then Bernoulli GLM, SLCP, SIR, LV.
    - Implement src/metrics.py.
    - Run NPSE for budgets 1000, 10000, 100000 and compare to sbibm references.
    - Generate Figure 2 analogues.

    PHASE 4: Density evaluation and TSNPSE
    - Implement src/density.py with Hutchinson trace estimator.
    - Implement src/hpr.py truncated proposal sampling.
    - Implement src/tsnpse.py.
    - First test TSNPSE on two-dimensional benchmarks, then SLCP/LV.
    - Verify HPR rejection sampling acceptance rate is not prohibitively low.
    - Generate Figure 3 analogues.

    PHASE 5: Prior score, NLSE, and SNPSE variants
    - Implement src/prior.py.
    - Implement src/nlse.py and compare with NPSE on selected benchmarks.
    - Implement src/snpse.py for SNPSE-A and SNPSE-B.
    - Implement SNPSE-C only if exact/estimated proposal prior scores are stable.
    - Reproduce Figures 5 and 6.

    PHASE 6: Real-world pyloric experiment
    - Implement experiments/run_pyloric.py only after TSNPSE is validated on
      benchmarks.
    - Add missing-data handling for invalid summary statistics.
    - Use 9 rounds, 30000 initial, 20000 per round.
    - Report valid summary rate, posterior predictive sample, and marginals.

    PHASE 7: Documentation and packaging
    - After all code is stable, create requirements.txt and README.md.
    - README should document:
        installation,
        SDE choices,
        commands for NPSE/TSNPSE/SNPSE,
        benchmark setup,
        expected results.

    MISSING DETAILS POLICY:
    - Use the standard score-matching weighting λ_t = g(t)² where the paper is
      ambiguous in the accessed text.
    - Use RK45 default tolerances from torchdiffeq.
    - For SBIBM tasks, prefer sbibm reference simulators and posteriors when exact
      implementations are complex.
    - For SNPSE-A proposal-prior density, implement the sample-based proposal-prior
      approximation described in Appendix C.2.3 when more than two rounds are used.
